# UMO JIRA Tracker — Orientation

This rule is active because `.umo/jira-tracker.json` is present. It reminds agents of the key conventions. The model itself lives in `references/work-tracking-model.md` at the plugin root.

## The work-tracking model

**Five issue types, nothing else:** **Flow** (level 2) → **Slice** (the epic slot) → **Task**, **Bug**, **Request** (level 0).

**Story and Sub-task do not exist.** Dev work is a Task, full stop; private breakdown is checkboxes or beads. If someone asks for either, say the type does not exist rather than substituting something.

**The parent chain is the only structural edge** — no flow or slice field to fill, and none to drift. `Blocks` is the only link type. Cross-project parents are normal: a Request lives in the provider's project and parents to the consumer's Slice.

**Unused fields — never read, never write:** Sprint, story points, original estimate / time tracking, Epic Link. Due date is the `needed-by` on a Request and is meaningless on a Task.

**Terminals:** match on `statusCategory`, never on the literal status name. A Task reaching Done means the PR merged and tests are green; a Request reaching its terminal means **consumer proof**, which only the consumer can declare. **Retired** is the global "withdrawn without being completed" terminal — its Resolution is required by policy (the live transition does not enforce it) and it is excluded from every count.

## What this plugin may do

- **Creates** Tasks and Bugs, under a Slice. Nothing else.
- **Transitions** Tasks and Bugs. Nothing else.
- **Pulls** all five types into Beads.

Flows, Slices and Requests are authored and moved by the flow PM and the tech leads, on the board — they carry demo statements, contracts, mock owners and due dates.

## Beads and Jira are not two views of one tree

The execution plan an agent follows *inside* one Task is an engineering artifact that lives in git. It is **not** synced to Jira; no bridge is built. Decompose freely with `bd create --parent`.

Only a **unit of assignment or delivery** — someone works on it for a meaningful stretch, or QA/PM/EM/CTO needs to see its status — becomes a Jira Task under the Slice. And it is a **sibling** of the current Task, never a child.

## Bead conventions

- **Title format**: `[{JIRA-KEY}] {JIRA summary}` — do not deviate, and keep the slice coordinate the summary carries.
- **Labels**: every synced bead carries `jira:{KEY}`. Look up beads via `bd list --label "jira:{KEY}" --json`.
- **Description zones**: the description has a JIRA-sourced zone (above `## Notes`) and a user/agent Notes zone (below `## Notes`). Sync only overwrites the JIRA zone; never touch `## Notes` and below.
- **Parent linkage**: Task/Bug/Request beads → Slice-bead; Slice-bead → Flow-bead. Use `bd dep add` or `--parent` to express this.

## Naming standard and the merge gate

- **Branch:** `{type}/{JIRA-KEY}-{short-description}` — types: `feat`, `fix`, `hotfix`, `chore`, `refactor`, `test`, `docs`, `ci`, `perf`, `build`; short-description 2–5 words kebab-case. (e.g. `feat/PAY-1234-add-kafka-retry`)
- **MR title:** `{type}(scope): lowercase imperative subject (JIRA-KEY)` — Conventional Commits with the key in parentheses at the **end**, which keeps the title commitlint-safe. (e.g. `feat(publisher): add kafka retry (PAY-1234)`)
- **Commit:** `type(scope): description` (Conventional Commits).
- **Jira summary grammar:** `[{SLICE-COORDINATE}] {outcome}` — Task is an imperative verb phrase, Bug is the symptom not the diagnosis. Dash in the coordinate, never slash.
- **The merge gate:** **no MR merges without a Jira Task, and the merge commit references the task.** The key is CI-enforced in the branch and MR title, and the merged-PR automation reads it to flip the Task to Done. Never create an MR without one.
- **Forbidden:** branches or MR titles without a JIRA key, names like `tmp`/`wip`/`test`, direct commits to `main`/`dev`/`release`, MRs without a description, and layer names, person names, status words or "part 2 / misc / fixes" in a Jira summary.

## MR creation rule

**Always use `/umo-jira-tracker:mr` to create Merge Requests.** Never call `glab mr create` or any GitLab MCP `create_merge_request` directly. The `/mr` command is the only entry point — it handles branch setup, commit organisation, MR preview, assignee, description template, and JIRA sync as a single approved flow. Invoking the underlying `gitlab-mr` skill directly bypasses these steps and produces inconsistent MRs (wrong title format, missing assignee, no JIRA update).

If you are mid-task and need to ship code to a branch, follow the phases in `commands/umo-jira-tracker:mr.md` rather than shelling out to `glab mr create` yourself.

## JIRA mutation rules

- **Never mutate JIRA without an explicit developer approval gate.** Always preview the comment body, the transition, or the new issue payload before calling any Atlassian MCP write tool.
- **Terminal states are one-way locally**: the sync closes a bead when its JIRA issue reaches Done, Closed or Retired, but never re-opens a bead that the developer closed locally.
- **Never transition on MR creation.** Task Done is the merged-PR automation's call.
- **Every created issue needs a Slice parent.** Creating one without a parent requires the exact phrase `create unlinked` plus a justification, and trips the org's orphan detector. Suggest the squad's `[TH-<KEY>-S0]` tech-health slice first.
- **Never set Retired to get an issue out of the way.** It means withdrawn without being completed, it needs a Resolution, and retiring a Slice cascades — which is why this plugin retires Tasks and Bugs only.

## Available commands

| Command | Purpose |
|---------|---------|
| `/umo-jira-tracker:setup` | First-run wizard |
| `/umo-jira-tracker:sync` | JIRA → Beads sync |
| `/umo-jira-tracker:work [KEY]` | Claim + discuss |
| `/umo-jira-tracker:create task\|bug` | Create a JIRA Task or Bug under a Slice |
| `/umo-jira-tracker:commit` | Conventional commits |
| `/umo-jira-tracker:mr` | Create MR + sync JIRA |
| `/umo-jira-tracker:close` | Close bead + JIRA Done |

## Config location

`.umo/jira-tracker.json` — run `/umo-jira-tracker:setup` to create or update.
